.TH NFLOG_DNS 8 "2026" "nflog_dns" "System Administration Commands"
.SH NAME
nflog_dns \- extract and log DNS replies from NFLOG netfilter group
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B nflog_dns
[\fIOPTION\fR]...
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B nflog_dns
captures DNS reply packets from a Linux netfilter NFLOG group and logs their
resource records and reply codes to syslog or standard output. The program
binds to an NFLOG group and processes incoming DNS response packets,
extracting domain names and their resolved values, or logging the DNS reply
code when a query was not answered successfully.
.PP
The
.B \-\-qtype
and
.B \-\-rcode
options select which DNS record types and reply codes are logged; see
.B OPTIONS
below for the full lists.
.PP
This tool is designed to work with iptables/ip6tables/nftables rules that send
DNS reply packets to an NFLOG group for logging purposes.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-f ", " \-\-facility=\fIFACILITY\fR
Set syslog facility for logging (default: user).
Valid facilities include: auth, authpriv, cron, daemon, ftp, kern, local0\-local7,
lpr, mail, news, syslog, user, uucp.
.TP
.BR \-g ", " \-\-group=\fINUM\fR
Bind to NFLOG group \fINUM\fR (default: 123).
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Display help message and exit.
.TP
.BR \-l ", " \-\-loglevel=\fILOGLEVEL\fR
Set log level for syslog logging (default: info).
Valid levels: trace, debug, info, warn, error, critical.
.TP
.BR \-q ", " \-\-qtype=\fIQTYPE\fR,...
Comma\-separated, case\-insensitive list of DNS record (query) types to log
from successful replies (default: A,AAAA). The first use of this option on
the command line replaces the default selection entirely; repeating the
option adds further types to the selection already made, so
.B \-\-qtype=A,AAAA \-\-qtype=MX,TXT
selects all four types.
.IP
Special type
.B ALL
(i.e.
.BR \-\-rcode=ALL ")"
selects all valid qtypes.
.IP
Valid types are: A, NS, MD, MF, CNAME, SOA, MB, MG, MR, NULL, WKS, PTR,
HINFO, MINFO, MX, TXT, RP, AFSDB, X25, ISDN, RT, NSAP, NSAP\-PTR, SIG, KEY,
PX, GPOS, AAAA, LOC, NXT, EID, NIMLOC, SRV, ATMA, NAPTR, KX, CERT, A6,
DNAME, SINK, OPT, APL, DS, SSHFP, IPSECKEY, RRSIG, NSEC, DNSKEY, DHCID,
NSEC3, NSEC3PARAM, or ALL to select everything.
.TP
.BR \-r ", " \-\-rcode=\fIRCODE\fR,...
Comma\-separated, case\-insensitive list of DNS reply codes to log (default:
NOERROR). Follows the same replace\-then\-accumulate rule as
.BR \-\-qtype .
.IP
Valid codes are: NOERROR, FORMERR, SERVFAIL, NXDOMAIN, NOTIMP, REFUSED,
YXDOMAIN, YXRRSET, NXRRSET, NOTAUTH, NOTZONE, or ALL.
.IP
Note that running with a custom
.B \-\-rcode
list that omits
.B NOERROR
(e.g.
.BR \-\-rcode=NXDOMAIN ")"
turns off all successful\-reply record logging and
.B \-\-qtype
selection will be ignored.
.TP
.BR \-s ", " \-\-syslog
Log DNS replies to syslog instead of standard output.
.TP
.BR \-u ", " \-\-user=\fIUSER\fR
Run as \fIUSER\fR after dropping root privileges (default: nobody).
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-version
Display version information and exit.
.SH EXAMPLES
.PP
Run nflog_dns with default settings (output to stdout):
.RS
.nf
sudo nflog_dns
.fi
.RE
.PP
Log to syslog with daemon facility and warning level:
.RS
.nf
sudo nflog_dns \-\-syslog \-\-facility=daemon \-\-loglevel=warn
.fi
.RE
.PP
Log A, AAAA and PTR records from NFLOG group 100:
.RS
.nf
sudo nflog_dns \-g 100 \-\-qtype=A,AAAA,PTR
.fi
.RE
.PP
Also log MX and TXT records, in addition to the default A and AAAA records:
.RS
.nf
sudo nflog_dns \-\-qtype=A,AAAA \-\-qtype=MX,TXT
.fi
.RE
.PP
Log successful replies as well as NXDOMAIN and SERVFAIL errors:
.RS
.nf
sudo nflog_dns \-\-rcode=NOERROR,NXDOMAIN,SERVFAIL
.fi
.RE
.PP
Log only NXDOMAIN and SERVFAIL errors, without logging any successful replies:
.RS
.nf
sudo nflog_dns \-\-rcode=NXDOMAIN,SERVFAIL
.fi
.RE
.SH IPTABLES SETUP
To capture DNS replies, configure iptables to send DNS reply packets to NFLOG group:
.PP
For IPv4:
.RS
.nf
sudo iptables \-A INPUT \-p udp \-\-sport 53 \-j NFLOG \-\-nflog\-group 123
.fi
.RE
.PP
For IPv6:
.RS
.nf
sudo ip6tables \-A INPUT \-p udp \-\-sport 53 \-j NFLOG \-\-nflog\-group 123
.fi
.RE
.SH NFTABLES SETUP
To capture DNS replies, configure nftables to send DNS reply packets to NFLOG group:
.RS
.nf
sudo nft add rule inet filter input udp sport 53 log group 123
.fi
.RE
.SH RUNTIME STATISTICS
nflog_dns gathers statistics on received packets. To display runtime packet
statistics, send a SIGUSR1 signal to the running process:
.RS
.nf
sudo pkill \-USR1 nflog_dns
.fi
.RE
.PP
Statistics are logged through the same output destination (syslog or stdout) configured
for DNS records. The statistics include:
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
Total packets received from NFLOG
.IP \(bu 2
Invalid/malformed packets
.IP \(bu 2
Valid DNS response packets
.IP \(bu 2
DNS error replies logged (accounting for the \-\-rcode/\-\-qtype filters)
.IP \(bu 2
DNS records logged (accounting for the \-\-qtype/\-\-rcode filters)
.RE
.PP
Example output:
.RS
.nf
Statistics: received_packets=1523 invalid_packets=2 dns_responses=1441 logged_errors=80 logged_records=3042
.fi
.RE
.PP
Final statistics are automatically logged when nflog_dns exits.
.SH SIGNALS
.TP
.B SIGTERM
Gracefully stop nflog_dns. Final statistics are logged before exit.
.TP
.B SIGHUP
Same as SIGTERM. Used by the init system to stop the service.
.TP
.B SIGINT
Same as SIGTERM. Triggered by Ctrl+C when running interactively.
.TP
.B SIGUSR1
Log current packet statistics without interrupting operation.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/default/nflog_dns
Default configuration file containing command\-line options for the service.
.TP
.I /usr/lib/systemd/system/nflog_dns.service
Systemd service unit file.
.TP
.I /etc/init.d/nflog_dns
SysV init script (for systems without systemd).
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Successful execution or normal termination.
.TP
.B 1
Error occurred (invalid arguments, nflog initialization failure, etc.).
.SH KNOWN ISSUES
Due to a bug in libtins library, IPv6 reverse DNS lookups (ip6.arpa PTR records)
may not get parsed correctly. See: https://github.com/mfontanini/libtins/issues/551
.SH REQUIREMENTS
.B nflog_dns
requires the following libraries:
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
libtins (packet parsing)
.IP \(bu 2
libnetfilter_log (NFLOG interface)
.IP \(bu 2
libspdlog (logging)
.IP \(bu 2
libfmt (string formatting)
.RE
